From c156b60f14988a9072e842cccbeb66ed3ba1c831 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 09:52:54 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xenlib: release dead toolstack domains on VIRQ_DOM_EXC

A guest that DomD creates with `xl create` is introduced to this
server (XS_INTRODUCE, 0007), and its store ring is mapped here. When
it was destroyed nothing told the server: xs_release_domain() ran
only on XS_RELEASE, which libxl sends only on a soft reset (which
Xen does not offer on Arm), not on `xl destroy`. The ring stayed
mapped, so Xen kept the domain dead with one page (`(null) --p--d`
in `xl list`), and its CONFIG_DOMU_MAX slot stayed used: on a 5-slot
board the fourth guest of a boot was refused with "domain limit 5
reached".

Do what the stock xenstored does (tools/xenstored/domain.c,
check_domains()): bind VIRQ_DOM_EXC and, on each event, look up every
toolstack-introduced domain still tracked. Xen sends this VIRQ when a
domain shuts down, when its kill leaves it dead and when it is
destroyed (xen/common/domain.c), to the xenstore domain, which is Dom0
here (set_xs_domain(dom0) in xen/arch/arm/domain_build.c).

- A domain the hypervisor reports dead (XEN_DOMINF_dying, which Xen sets
  only for DOMDYING_dead) or gone (-ESRCH) is released, and
  "@releaseDomain" is fired. A domain that has only shut down is still
  there and is left alone.
- The event callback runs in the event-channel ISR, so it only wakes a
  thread. The release takes dl_mutex, joins the guest's xenstore worker
  and frees memory; it runs on its own thread, not the system workqueue,
  because a wedged worker can block that join for good. The port is
  bound with the event ISR off, so no event can reach a half-set handler
  entry.
- Each domain is asked for with XEN_DOMCTL_getdomaininfo, which answers
  for exactly that domid. A truncated sysctl list would make a live
  domain look gone.
- A scan also runs after each XS_INTRODUCE, for a domain that died
  before it was added to the list.
- A lookup that fails with an error other than -ESRCH is tried again a
  second later: if that was the domain's last VIRQ, no later event comes
  for it while its ring stays mapped. Only the first failure of a run is
  logged as a warning.

The release path changes for both callers:
- A domain can now be released twice (the event and an XS_RELEASE).
  f_released makes the second a no-op; before, a second release while a
  borrower held the domain dropped the borrower's reference.
- A release while the domain was borrowed was deferred and never
  completed: put_domain() and unref_domain() only decremented for a
  toolstack domain. The drop of the last reference now tears it down (on
  the borrower's thread). Until then an XS_INTRODUCE of that domid is
  refused (-EBUSY) instead of being taken as a duplicate.
- Only toolstack-introduced domains are released. An XS_RELEASE that
  named a Zephyr-created domain dropped Zephyr's state and left the
  hypervisor domain running with no owner.
- Only the nodes the guest owns are removed (stop_domain_stored()), as
  the stock xenstored does on a release. /local/domain/<id>, /vm/<uuid>
  and /libxl/<id> are owned by Dom0 and are libxl's, which removes them
  before it destroys the domain.
- The console is no longer stopped: this Dom0 never starts one for a
  toolstack domain, and stopping it logged an error on every release.
  The backend removal is dropped too: it did nothing for these domains,
  which this Dom0 sets up no backends for.
- The CONFIG_DOMU_MAX slot is counted free only once the guest's worker
  has been joined and its stack slot given back, so an XS_INTRODUCE that
  passes the check can start its own worker.
- The guest's store event channel is unbound with the event ISR off, on
  release and on a failed start, as the VIRQ is bound: the unbind writes
  the handler and then its argument, and an event in between would run
  the default handler with the wrong argument.
- "@releaseDomain" is fired through xss_notify_release_domain().
- Correct the comments that said `xl destroy` sends XS_RELEASE.

CONFIG_XEN_DOM_EXC_RELEASE (default y) turns the event on. Without it,
or if the bind fails (for example because another domain is the
xenstore domain), dead domains are released only on XS_RELEASE, with
the release path above.

Link: https://github.com/automotive-grade-linux/sodev-demo-workspace-rpi/issues/9
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
